      "narrative": "You push through the Copper Finch's heavy door and step out into the grey morning. The rain has softened to a fine mist that clings to your cloak and beads on your lashes. The street is narrow, cobblestones slick and dark.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises at the street's end\u2014a cracked black arch standing alone against the grey sky. No wall flanks it, no building supports it. It simply *stands*, a wound in the world framed in worked stone. As you draw closer, the air grows stiller, the mist thinning as if afraid to touch the arch itself.\n\nA figure stands beside the gate. A courier in a travel-stained cloak, hood drawn up against the damp. They're studying the sealed arch with the particular stillness of someone who's been standing there a while\u2014not passing through, but *waiting*.\n\nThe gate's surface is a solid sheet of pale, veined stone\u2014recently set, still raw at the edges. Fresh chisel marks score the seal's surface. A file's work, coarse and hurried. Below them, near the base, something dark and uneven stains the stone.\n\nAnd crumpled beside the gate's left pillar, half-hidden in shadow\u2014a stained canvas sack, its drawstring loose, spilling a few scraps of what looks like dried root or herb onto the wet cobbles.\n\nThe courier turns as they hear your approach. A woman's face beneath the hood\u2014pale, watchful, with tired eyes.\n\n*She says nothing. Just watches you approach the sealed gate.*\n\nWhat do you do?"
    },
