      "narrative": "You step closer to the sealed arch, the mist curling around your boots. The courier watches but doesn't move or speak\u2014just a silent witness at the edge of the scene.\n\nYou draw a breath and call out, voice carrying across the empty stretch of wet cobbles:\n\n**\"Old Bram!\"**\n\nThe name echoes off the stone, off the shuttered facades of the nearest buildings\u2014then fades into the grey.\n\nSilence.\n\nThe mist swallows the echo. No answering voice. No shuffle of feet. Just the faint drip of water from the gate's black arch, and the distant, muffled clatter of the Copper Finch's kitchen behind you.\n\nThe courier shifts her weight, the movement small but deliberate. Her voice, when it comes, is low and rough-edged from disuse:\n\n\"He won't answer. He's dead.\"\n\nA pause. She gestures with her chin toward the dark stain at the base of the sealed stone.\n\n\"Found him right there. Three mornings ago. Face-down against the seal, like he'd been trying to claw through.\"\n\nShe pulls her cloak tighter, her eyes not leaving the gate.\n\n\"You knew him?\"\n\n*Her question hangs in the damp air.*..."
