      "narrative": "You crouch beneath the arch, scanning the wet cobbles. Your eyes trace the dark stain at the base of the seal, follow the cracks in the stone, sweep across the scattered debris. The mist makes everything look like a clue\u2014every shadow a shape, every glint a thing of meaning.\n\nYour gaze passes over the crumpled canvas sack beside the left pillar. The drawstring hangs loose, a few dried roots or herbs spilled onto the wet stone. You glance at it\u2014but your eyes slide across its stained folds without pausing, dismissing it as nothing more than litter. A traveller's discarded bundle, maybe. Not worth a second look.\n\nYou straighten, turning back to the gate, and the question slips out half-formed:\n\n\"What's hidden here?\"\n\nThe courier's boots scrape against stone as she shifts. She doesn't answer immediately\u2014just watches you with those tired, patient eyes.\n\n\"Hidden?\" she repeats, the word carrying a note of dry amusement. \"Nothing's hidden. It's sealed. That's the point.\"\n\nShe steps closer, the motion unhurried, and gestures at the fresh chisel marks scoring the pale stone.\n\n\"Three days ago, this gate was open. People came and went.\" Her hand drops. \"Now it's shut. The Guild put the seal on it the morning they found Bram.\"\n\nShe looks at you sidelong.\n\n\"If something's hidden, it's not *under* the arch. It's in why he died trying to get *through* it.\"\n\n*Her words settle. The gate looms. The damp cold seeps through your cloak. What do you do?*..."
